{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/370416-how-common-are-monsters","id":370416,"title":"How common are \"monsters\"?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Hunter: The Vigil"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-01-14T17:43:43-08:00","id":370416,"title":"How common are \"monsters\"?","content":"This is a question I am sure is bandied about a lot, but permit me to ask it again: How common is the supernatural in the World of Darkness?<br /> <br /> This isn't a question I expect an authoritative answer to; I'm asking here for how folks have done it in their own games. How common is magic, big and small? How likely is someone to stumble across it? I ask this in part as a general nWoD question but also for Hunter in particular.<br /> <br /> Are you likely to walk into your local occult odds-and-ends store and find some odd talisman? How often in a city do you hear of strange deaths? Could a Hunter who is particularly alert find a new supernatural creature every day? Or do they typically go years without seeing something odd? How common are both &quot;big&quot; and &quot;small&quot; types of magic? Do you find people all over the place with small, odd abilities - perhaps not even aware of them - if you know how to look?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-14T18:06:49-08:00","id":370481,"title":"","content":"It may seem like a cop-out, but it's as common as you need.<br /> <br /> The writing about the stuff seems to suggest that nearly everyone has experienced at least one supernatural or weird event in their lives.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-14T18:22:11-08:00","id":370502,"title":"","content":"A fair enough answer. I suppose I could refine the question to suggest &quot;How common should it be to maintain a feeling of a dark world with odd things laying just under the surface?&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-14T18:33:05-08:00","id":370517,"title":"","content":"I think the cop-out answer applies there as well, humorously. If your PCs are groaning and rolling their eyes because every session is a Monster of the Week episode then you're definitely undermining the game's mood yourself.<br /> <br /> On the other hand some games have different levels. To me, <b>Vampire</b>'s night life is teeming with blood-sucking, soul-devouring, flesh-eating, memory-consuming predators that potentially lurk behind every dark shadow - Kindred or not - but that's a <i>mood</i>. That's not a presented outline of every creature and its abilities and motives. It's about the creeping terror that, as a mortal, something is in that sketchy alley and you're its prey.<br /> <br /> Especially in the 2.0 edition the books definitely imply that mortals <i>know</i> or <i>mostly believe/guess</i> in the supernatural but they <i>don't talk about it</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-14T18:51:36-08:00","id":370565,"title":"","content":"To be perfectly honest, this is more a question that comes to me because of Mage/Changeling than Hunter, far more the former than the latter, but it felt appropriate to ask here both because of the nature of the question and that it arose when reading Hunter books. I'm wondering if it's possible to create a setting where those &quot;in the know&quot; might just be able to say &quot;If you need to know the future, knock three times on the door behind Chen's Fish and Chips, and tell them the White Raven sent you.&quot;, or some such, and still have magic feel hidden and strange.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-14T18:57:09-08:00","id":370574,"title":"","content":"Well as far as critters go in our games we go like this when it comes to numbers. Vampires&gt;werewolves&gt;mages&gt;changelings&gt;mummies&gt;demo ns&gt;sin eaters&gt;prometheans. If you want hard numbers again this are only approximations. You could say that there is approximately one vampire per 100.000-200.000 humans and mages 1 per 1.000.000 humans. However it depends on how common you want supernatural events to be.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2964-firanai","name":"Firanai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2964_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"672"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-14T22:57:05-08:00","id":370771,"title":"","content":"Also keep in mind that pretty much every playable supernatural has some kind of rule about keeping vanilla mortals in the dark as much as possible, be it the Masquerade, the Veil, or &quot;The Herd Must Not Know.&quot; So the sort of monsters and whatnot that ends up on the radar of hunters tend to be those who <i>haven't</i> been as quiet and careful as their peers might like. At which point the concerns of the would-be victim's fellow supernaturals turn from &quot;They can't be allowed to know anything about us!&quot; to &quot;They can't find out anything about any of us <i>beyond <u>that guy</u>!&quot;</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/747-knowbody","name":"Knowbody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-14T23:54:32-08:00","id":370790,"title":"","content":"As long as you keep pressenting it in a justified light, you can do whatever you please. I have an example.<br /> In a Hunter scenario I'm part of, the Storyteller made the mistake of allowing us to stumble upon Demon, after Mage, after Conspiracy after Ghost, after Werewolf, after Occultist, after Criptid, after Spirit, and it just seemed like things were so laughably common that it made the entire &quot;This is a secret!&quot; seem like a bad sell.<br /> On the other hand, I run a Sin-Eater scenario myself, in which I've introduced a Cabal of Mages and a family of Vampires all within a relatively short time. The Vampires were contacted by the Sin-Eaters themselves (&quot;Hey look! That dude is dead! Lets go talk to him!&quot;), and the Mages contacted the Sin-Eaters after they had caused some loud metaphysical 'noise' with a Ghost. I dont think this undermined the secretive part of it, because the only reason these things happened, was because the Sin-Eaters allready have a foot firmly steeped in the realms of supernaturality.<br /> <br /> So to bring it back to a Hunter relevant scenario; Make sure events tie together. If The Cell encounters some Mages, some Ghosts/Spirits and some Werewolfs, all within the same month and entirely disconnected from each other, thats gonna seem cheap. But if they encounter some Mages, who gets pissed at the Hunters for interfering in their work, who then sends some Ghosts/Spirits to kill the Hunters, and the Werewolfs are enemies of the Mages, wishing to ally themselves with the Hunters to bring down the Mages, then it doesn't seem so cheap, since one event acted as a trigger for everything else. Potentially, everything can be tied together like this. As long as the trigger event was the same, you can hinge almost as much as you please on that. Its the disconnected triggers, thats gonna cheapen the deal for people, I believe.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1958-krobeles","name":"Krobeles","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1958_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-15T00:31:10-08:00","id":370793,"title":"","content":"I usually go &quot;rare, but influential&quot;. In a large city, New York for example, 20 vampires can still have a pretty sizable impact on the experience for most humans: three or four reasonably powerful vampires manage to ghoul/Dominate/whatever a few middling-to-high officials, use the influence to affect building zones and suchforth. Roughly the same number of mages will cause plenty of friction, given mages tendencies to work together only reluctantly (even the Conventions or whatever have plenty of internal friction), and so on. Say 10 Bound, 10 Uratha (and 15 Pure), 15 Changelines and maybe 100 Misc gives a supernatural to human ratio of roughly 1:85,000.<br /> <br /> This ratio has the advantage of having the supernatural numbers low enough that finding them is difficult for Hunters, and makes the players pretty important: 3 to 6 out of a population of 20 is a significant proportion. And players should always be important in how games unfold, otherwise what's the point of the game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-15T04:37:52-08:00","id":370901,"title":"","content":"You can also use more of the &quot;minor&quot; templates in the everyday hunt, like going after slashers, rogues lucifuges, inferno contractors ..., and keep encounters with the majors template for the climax ^^<br /> <br /> This way, &quot;corrupted humans&quot; would be somewhat common, but the really Big Bad Guys are the rarest.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3883-arabidopsis","name":"Arabidopsis","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-15T06:40:45-08:00","id":370929,"title":"","content":"With the story I'm running, I have set it in my hometown of Winnipeg, which is the largest city in the province and the capital. It has a relatively well-known reputation for strange and unusual things, and so I've represented that accordingly by having people with minor supernatural powers be fairly common, and people are at least marginally aware of the supernatural. Ghosts are pretty well believed in- there's a room at a hotel downtown that is always booked for 8 months straight because it's the most haunted in the hotel. Most people know that the Legislative Building, the provincial capital, was built according to occult mathematics. In my game, it's an open secret among the Unchained that it's a God-Machine Facility, but no one wants to get close enough to find out what.<br /> <br /> Perhaps more importantly, because of how spread out everything in the province is, and how important having lots of people around is to concealing most supernatural beings, there's a pretty high concentration of them. I think the most numerous I have are minor supernaturals like stigmatics, then Changelings, then Mages. Though, to be fair, the Changelings and Mages sometimes even come from different provinces. It's a weird city, with a lot of different powerful supernatural crap going on- they can't help but come. More importantly for the game (and unfortunately for real life) there's a pretty high rate of violent crime and missing people. 5,000 people go missing yearly, and about 250 <i>stay</i> missing- more or less. My personal opinion is that real life should help write the story. Based on where you're setting the game, and what goes on in that town, and what it's like around it, how much supernatural weirdness can you get away with before any semblance of a Masquerade is gone?<br /> <br /> Even by my, probably, generous numbers, the overtly supernatural community forms only about 0.14<sup>%</sup> of the population of the city. So, it really depends on what kind of story you want to run.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":4152,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","user":"Arcanist","date":". 01-15-2015, 09:50 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-15T09:00:01-08:00","id":371094,"title":"","content":"I agree very heavily with the last part of what Arcanist said. It really depends on what kind of story you're trying to tell. Part of the emphasis I want in my own Geist chroronicle, is that the Sin-Eater exist as gestalts, in a quasi-state between two worlds, which are unable or unwilling to reconcile each other on their own. I want this thematic to echo out throughout the different supernatural groups in London, all of whom have a tenuous, but intensely xenophobic and fearful peace with each other with alot of unresolved hate and animosity even amongst themselves. Sort of a laissez faire approach to diplomacy and peace. Only the Sin-Eaters really understands or appriciates that a middle and common ground is needed for everything to work smoothly.<br /> If I want to run with this thematic, I need the supernaturals to be somewhat more pleantyful than they might otherwise be. If theres only about 20 of them total in the city, the thematic falls immensely flat.<br /> <br /> However, in a typical (perhaps typical is a wierd word, given Hunters notoriously varied gameplay) Hunter story, this isn't a thematic you can really run with, and neither is it a thematic you'de want to run with, if you had the chance. Ultimately, I think it comes down to figuring out what sort of thematic and/or story you want, and populating your setting accordingly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1958-krobeles","name":"Krobeles","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1958_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-17T02:27:58-08:00","id":373437,"title":"","content":"In my city, I have a few rough numbers. 70 (Used to be 120 but Strix happened) Kindred in the city, 65 Uratha (3/4ths Forsaken due to the Pure pinning everyone in the city), 45 Awakened (Including Seers and Left handed), 5 Prometheans, 80 Lost, 30 Sin eaters, and around 20 Demons. There are also 15 Arisen but only 3 are active at any given time.<br /> <br /> Hunter Cells (as in plural) number around 80 (3-7 members each) and the vast majority of the time, they rarely deal with the known supernaturals types at all as they are either devoted to researching endowments, working their professions, or going after stuff that WoD throws out as a matter of course. I would say about only about half actually know of the known supernatural types while around 24 cells actually work on dealing with them (through conflict or trade) on a regular basis.<br /> <br /> The modern mega city is a freakin' huge place. Weird shit happens all the time and there's more than enough space in the sandbox where Hunters can hunt weird shit without dealing with any of the splats at all.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/604-shock","name":"Shock","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar604_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1071"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-17T07:11:39-08:00","id":373448,"title":"","content":"I think that as a general rule a 0.5-1 % of the population of a big city as supernatural beings is a good estimate. At least that's the rule we usually follow.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2964-firanai","name":"Firanai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2964_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"672"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-17T11:54:37-08:00","id":373645,"title":"","content":"Do you think there's more inhuman stuff? Lab rats with superhuman, evil intelligence, sentient oozes and murder trees?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-17T12:53:16-08:00","id":373697,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Firanai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/370416-how-common-are-monsters?p=373448#post373448\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that as a general rule a 0.5-1 % of the population of a big city as supernatural beings is a good estimate. At least that's the rule we usually follow.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you divide that to <b>all</b> supernaturals in city, I could agree with that, only as very rought estimination, with something more like 0.1-0,05% ( one sup per 1000-2000 people ). Remeber we have now, like, what, 9 &quot;races&quot;. with Beasts coming this year to roll in the metaphorical ( and literal ) town. And that Big Three ( vampires, werewolves and mages ) will probably have something like twice as much as rest of monsters in given region, having strongest presence in all and complex societies.<br /> <br /> For example, in London Urban area we have something like 10 millions of people. So my estimations of about 500 mages, about maybe the same of vampires and about 100 werewolves in the City are not something outside of setting logic. Even considering changelings, demons, prometheans and all the rest of supernatural communites.<br /> London is crowded city, it can have dense populations.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, my home city of Lublin is now something like 350 000 citizens now. I could not envision here like 300 mages, but can something along the way 50 mages, 20 vampires, 30 werewolves and for dozen per other beings. Or even more logical would be to have like 150 mages, but in the whole <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lublin_Voivodeship\" target=\"_blank\">voievodship</a>, scatered largely in country, but highest density in the Lublin.<br /> <br /> And it's also the way why I think coming of something like 25 changelings in small New England town of 2500 citizens is Lost population explosion, unprocedent event.<br /> <br /> Also, we had very <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/267768-fwc-weekly-discussion-magical-society?p=268059#post268059\">similar discussion</a> with Magical Society spoiler for 2ed Mage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1437,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","user":"wyrdhamster","date":". 01-17-2015, 03:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-17T17:46:22-08:00","id":373936,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/370416-how-common-are-monsters?p=373697#post373697\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And it's also the way why I think coming of something like 25 changelings in small New England town of 2500 citizens is Lost population explosion, unprocedent event.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually Changeling population has no direct correlation to mortal population. Despite their populations of mortals running in the millions, the New York and Chicago area only have 37 (for NYC's 8.4 million) and 21 changelings (for Chicago's 9.7 million). Meanwhile Los Angeles, with its population of 3.8 million, has around 400 changelings.<br /> <br /> And of course, pretty much everywhere there are spirits, just on the other side of the Gauntlet, able to reach over and possess people at a moments notice. And demons, and ghosts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 01-17-2015, 09:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-18T00:29:18-08:00","id":374099,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/370416-how-common-are-monsters?p=373936#post373936\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually Changeling population has no direct correlation to mortal population. Despite their populations of mortals running in the millions, the New York and Chicago area only have 37 (for NYC's 8.4 million) and 21 changelings (for Chicago's 9.7 million). Meanwhile Los Angeles, with its population of 3.8 million, has around 400 changelings.</div> </div> </div> </div>Where you get those numbers. I marked mine as made up for plays, on rought estimations, but those are just random. Only 37 changeling for 8.5 millions?! It's almost impossible low ( if you goes with standards ) - unless it's your home campaign assumption, but should not be pointed as general idea for setting building. Exception to the rule.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-18T00:38:39-08:00","id":374103,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/370416-how-common-are-monsters?p=374099#post374099\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Where you get those numbers. I marked mine as made up for plays, on rought estimations, but those are just random. Only 37 changeling for 8.5 millions?! It's almost impossible low ( if you goes with standards ) - unless it's your home campaign assumption, but should not be pointed as general idea for setting building. Exception to the rule.</div> </div> </div> </div>I see what you're going for, but near I can tell there is no rule to have an exception to. Trends, maybe, but that's about the best you can reasonably claim, and not even very strong or well-known ones, given how often this question gets asked and the range we've seen on this very thread, not to mention other threads.<br /> <br /> Minor quibble, I know.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-18T08:13:15-08:00","id":374280,"title":"","content":"I'm pretty sure that vampires are the only supernatural where there's pretty firm population ratios for an area, because it's based around how many vampires can regularly feed on the human population night after night without drawing undue attention. There's no hard and fast rules about how many people in an area will Awaken or go through the First Change or even end up disappearing to Fae!Arcadia (I don't even want to speculate about Sin-Eaters). The number of werewolves in the area might be determined more by the state of the local Shadow, while mages tend to congregate in numbers around sites of supernatural mysteries.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/747-knowbody","name":"Knowbody","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"391"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-18T09:51:22-08:00","id":374302,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/370416-how-common-are-monsters?p=374099#post374099\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Where you get those numbers. I marked mine as made up for plays, on rought estimations, but those are just random. Only 37 changeling for 8.5 millions?! It's almost impossible low ( if you goes with standards ) - unless it's your home campaign assumption, but should not be pointed as general idea for setting building. Exception to the rule.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Like I said, changelings have no direct correlation to mortal populations. See, while vampires spread their illness, and werewolves and mages are created from a portion of the population, changelings are taken by chance. And after that, it's even more random chance whether a changeling will ever escape from the Hedge to make it back into the world, especially since some cities have easier pathways to Arcadia than others.. This is why you don't have a steady % of a city's population as changelings, because the city's population doesn't matter.<br /> <br /> Those numbers are from the Changeling book, <i>Lords of Summer</i>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 01-18-2015, 12:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-18T12:14:35-08:00","id":374365,"title":"","content":"As Nofather says, it's as equally possible to have a massive city with a Changeling population in the dozens as it is to have a small town out in the bush with a solid 40% of its population made up of Changelings (the Fetch density in such a location could get pretty nightmarish). While the rule &quot;however many you need for the story&quot; is one that gets trotted out for near every line, for Changeling it's an in-game setting assumption. Changeling numbers in a region could literally be dictated by the number the Wyrd deems necessary for a story, and as GM you are the game's stand-in for the Wyrd.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-18T12:23:46-08:00","id":374367,"title":"","content":"But there is also all &quot;the supernaturals are secret&quot; shtick. There is certain numbers in given location after what presence of werewolves, proofs of magic or even signs of fae existence are obvious. If population of supernaturals is too big in one location, mortals/Sleepers will sooner or later know they are there. I think 1 per 1000-2000 is good ratio for that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-18T12:32:49-08:00","id":374371,"title":"","content":"World of Darkness takes for granted that people already do know (or at the least suspect, or knew once and forgot) there's weird stuff out there, they just don't pry into it because weird stuff is also usually dangerous stuff. Vampires are more interested in keeping the Masquerade because of the individuals it can get in trouble. It's one thing for the world to believe there might be vampires. It's another to believe Tom, on the corner, is a vampire who sucks peoples blood. Because there are people who stand for a light in the darkness, who aren't afraid to face the darkness fight back, and when Tom brags about being a vampire on Facebook, some Hunter is going to go shove a stake in his chest.<br /> <br /> But changelings aren't werewolves, who might fight a bloody battle in the middle of a street, or vampires who drink the blood of people and occasionally leave bodies behind. Their method of gaining glamour is invisible, their Mask disguises them perfectly to the vast majority of people, and most of their powers are covert or subtle, things that cannot be seen in the first place.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 01-18-2015, 03:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-18T21:17:03-08:00","id":374843,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Knowbody</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/370416-how-common-are-monsters?p=374280#post374280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm pretty sure that vampires are the only supernatural where there's pretty firm population ratios for an area, because it's based around how many vampires can regularly feed on the human population night after night without drawing undue attention. There's no hard and fast rules about how many people in an area will Awaken or go through the First Change or even end up disappearing to Fae!Arcadia (I don't even want to speculate about Sin-Eaters). The number of werewolves in the area might be determined more by the state of the local Shadow, while mages tend to congregate in numbers around sites of supernatural mysteries.</div> </div> </div> </div>Interesting you'd say that, because I've always thought that Sin-Eaters are one of the easier ones to predict trends, if not specific numbers. See, people who die of old age never become Bound, because that's the one death that the geist cannot reverse. So, if we assume that includes death from things like heart/organ failure, Alzheimers and other things that are basically &quot;body wearing down&quot;, that means you'd expect to see more opportunity (and thus, all being equal, more Bound) in places where deaths from violence, starvation etc are more common, relatively speaking. Oddly enough, those are also likely the places you'd <i>expect</i> to see more ghosts and suchforth.<br /> <br /> So, for example, in the city where I live in Australia, which has pretty low crime rate and very low rates of starvation and suicide and the like, you wouldn't expect to see as many as, say, a war-torn area in Africa, one of those places where the Four Horsemen like to vacation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-21T10:15:40-08:00","id":377338,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But there is also all &quot;the supernaturals are secret&quot; shtick. </div> </div> </div>It's less that they are hidden from humanity and more because humanity as a species is in denial of their existence. It's something like a psychological self defense mechanism, people prefer not to poke in the darkness if they can avoid that. Plus there are still regions in the world where people do believe that supernaturals exists, for example if that's part of local beliefs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cI am absolute, I am perfect, I am supreme. I shall be eternal. My tragedy, is that there is no other fate for me. My powerlessness was that I couldn\u2019t subjugate my journey to the gods, while dreaming of rebirth at the end of distant time, like other pharaohs.\u201d Ramesses II, Fate/Prototype: Argent Fragments.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2918-kat","name":"Kat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2918_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1762"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-22T11:04:39-08:00","id":378213,"title":"","content":"When I run games my supernaturals tend to know of each other, but not necessarily know about each other. A vampire who owns a night club is going to rub shoulders with a lot of people, not all of whom are here for the dancing. A Werewolf who runs a pawn shop will likely meet a few ghouls, mages and hunters. You can only spend so much time in a city before you start to know the people who live in it. After 50 years a moderately social Vampire will have seen and been involved in a lot of things. The same could be said for a Werewolf, whose lifespan is practically doubled.<br /> <br /> What does this mean for hunters and supernatural populations? Just as many mages are likely to be in a shoddy garage band as a sleeper. A werewolf is just as likely to be in your yoga class as anyone else. I think that even with a small population (my current game has less than 50 supernaturals in a city of over 250,000) chances are they are somewhat connected to one another, even if it is via five degrees of separation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2861-nyx","name":"Nyx","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2861_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"261"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}